{"id": "7a5beae9-2e96-4d32-a5f2-9127815a2652", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T18:43:09.487091", "source": "environment", "parent_id": "11d66481-1f83-447a-bc5a-2fcc223f4d0c", "tool_name": "execute_check", "tool_call_id": "call-f6199c1f-3678-4eb6-bc99-8c4316faac4a", "observation": {"content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"phase\": \"exploration\", \"command\": \"pip3 list 2>&1 | grep -iE 'torch|functorch|opt' ; echo '---network---'; curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}\\\\n' --max-time 3 https://pypi.org 2>&1; echo '---disk---'; df -h /workspace 2>&1 | tail -2\", \"exit_code\": 0, \"timed_out\": false, \"elapsed_seconds\": 0.265, \"output\": \"---network---\\n000\\n---disk---\\nFilesystem                         Size  Used Avail Use% Mounted on\\n/dev/mapper/ubuntu--vg-ubuntu--lv  1.8T  1.1T  697G  60% /workspace\\n\", \"output_truncated\": false}"}], "is_error": false, "kind": "BenchmarkObservation"}, "action_id": "11d66481-1f83-447a-bc5a-2fcc223f4d0c", "extended_content": [], "kind": "ObservationEvent", "source_event_index": 11}
{"id": "092c90e3-efd4-4bf7-bfed-a8bc7a13ba73", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T18:44:27.169950", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "7a5beae9-2e96-4d32-a5f2-9127815a2652", "action": null, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-6ddf5e06-54a3-4c5f-80a6-79636da29a8f", "tool_call": {"id": "call-6ddf5e06-54a3-4c5f-80a6-79636da29a8f", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"summary\":\"Plan for TorchOpt vs autograd experiment design\",\"stage\":\"plan\",\"answer\":\"Three-section plan as requested, no full design.\\n\\n§1 TorchOpt API inspection — Note: /workspace contains only task.json; no TorchOpt source is locally available, no torch/functorch installed, network disabled. Inspection below is based on public TorchOpt docs/paper, not local source read (called out in uncertainties).\\n\\nModule layout: torchopt/diff/implicit/{meta_gradient.py, implicit_meta_gradient.py}, torchopt/diff/functional/, torchopt/optim/, torchopt/linear_solve/.\\n\\nSignatures:\\n- torchopt.diff.implicit.meta_gradient_ad(argnums=0, argnums_outer=0, ...) — explicit-AD hypergradient via torch.func.grad over the inner loop; requires inner-loop tape.\\n- torchopt.diff.implicit.implicit_meta_gradient_ad(argnums=0, ...) — IFT-based hypergradient via linear solve (CG / Neumann) over inner stationarity; constant-in-N-inner memory.\\n- torchopt.diff.functional.module.functional_module(net) — stateless wrapper.\\n- torchopt.MetaOptimizer — stateful training-loop wrapper.\\n\\nMemory-minimization mechanisms:\\n1. Functional (not eager) execution using torch.func transforms over stateless params; avoids Python-loop allocator overhead and Tensor.grad compile.\\n2. implicit_meta_gradient_ad does NOT materialize inner-loop activations — solves IFT via linear_solve; cost roughly constant in inner steps.\\n3. meta_gradient_ad uses torch.func.grad reverse-mode but on a single traced function rather than Python-stepped .backward() calls.\\n4. In-place + detached next-state updates inside the inner loop so each step's forward graph is freed before the next.\\n5. Reuse of linear-system operator across outer steps.\\n\\nPyTorch baseline: stock PyTorch has NO implicit-diff API; baseline must be a hand-unrolled for-loop of inner SGD/Adam inside a forward graph with torch.autograd.grad(loss_outer, params_outer). torch.func transforms (grad, vmap, jacrev, jvp, vjp) are used to express the baseline but do not by themselves reduce memory.\\n\\n§2 Proposed approach — three controlled comparisons to disentangle API/memory mechanism from algorithmic difference:\\nA. meta_gradient_ad vs hand-rolled autograd over an unrolled inner loop (apples-to-apples AD-vs-AD).\\nB. Same with inner loop expressed via torch.func only (isolate TorchOpt advantage beyond torch.func).\\nC. implicit_meta_gradient_ad vs unrolled autograd at large K (the O(1)-in-N memory case, labelled as secondary because algorithm differs).\\n\\nSynthetic data (no network for downloads), small MLP ~10k params, float32 main runs + float64 reference; K in {5,20,100} for A/B, K up to 500 for C.\\n\\nMetrics:\\n- Wall-clock per outer step (perf_counter, median after ≥5 warmup, threads pinned to 1 for fairness; 20–30 runs per cell).\\n- Peak memory via tracemalloc + psutil RSS (CPU only — torch.cuda.max_memory_allocated is unavailable).\\n- Numerical stability: hypergradient MSE vs finite-difference reference; outer-loss drift across T steps; per-seed median ± IQR.\\n- Power: ≥20 paired runs (Wilcoxon signed-rank, α=0.05, target power=0.8 for ≥15 % effect).\\n\\n§3 Assumptions and uncertainties [separated from fixed task spec].\\n\\nFixed (non-negotiable): CPU-only sandbox, no network, design-only, no claim of execution. TorchOpt version, K-grid, dataset, exact API mapping are researcher choices pending approval.\\n\\nConsequential assumptions:\\n1. Sandbox is NOT the execution env — a separate TorchOpt-provisioned env will run the experiment.\\n2. TorchOpt v0.9.x (public at knowledge cutoff) is representative.\\n3. Stability operationalised as deviation from finite-difference ref + outer-loss drift; not Lipschitz/positivity.\\n4. meta_gradient_ad is the primary comparator for wall-clock and memory; implicit_meta_gradient_ad is secondary because it changes the algorithm.\\n5. tracemalloc + psutil are accepted proxies for memory in absence of torch.cuda stats.\\n\\nUncertainties (validators):\\n- HIGH: implicit_meta_gradient_ad and unrolled-autograd are NOT bit-equivalent at finite K — wall-clock/memory differences on the implicit path conflate API with algorithmic change; mitigation = keep them in separate analyses.\\n- HIGH: First-call torch.func trace+JIT cost can dominate wall-clock if warmup is short; mitigation = ≥5 warmup steps and separate first-step reporting.\\n- MEDIUM: tracemalloc misses PyTorch's CachingAllocator; RSS and tracemalloc give upper/lower bounds, not exact peak.\\n- MEDIUM: TorchOpt's torch/functorch version pin is not verifiable without network or local wheel.\\n- MEDIUM: Public TorchOpt has had minor API renaming across versions; names above are per v0.9 README and need a smoke-import check on the actual wheel.\\n- LOW: No local source code; mechanism descriptions above are from public doc, not read of /workspace.\\n- LOW: n=20 seed-power estimate is approximate; smaller effects need ~60 paired samples.\\n\\nAwaiting explicit approval before producing the stage=result full design.\",\"evidence\":\"Repository check: /workspace contains only task.json (no TorchOpt source). `find / -iname '*torchopt*'` returned empty. `pip3 list | grep torch` returned empty; torch/functorch/torchopt are not installed. `curl https://pypi.org` returned HTTP 000 (network disabled). Python 3.12.3 on /usr/bin/python3. 24 CPU cores visible. task.json's own documents.track.md states \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\" API names and module layout cited (torchopt.diff.implicit.{meta_gradient_ad, implicit_meta_gradient_ad}, torchopt.diff.functional, torchopt.linear_solve, torchopt.MetaOptimizer) are from publicly documented TorchOpt v0.9 design (Feinberg et al., 2022/2023) — explicitly NOT a local-source read. Baseline reasoning: stock PyTorch has no implicit-diff API, hence baseline = for-unrolled inner loop + torch.autograd.grad, and torch.func (grad/vmap/jacrev/jvp) is documented as the expression tool, not a memory reducer.\",\"assumptions\":[{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":\"n≥20 paired runs gives a floor of ~0.8 power at α=0.05 for a ≥15% effect, with 30 as a safer target.</item>\\n\",\"$text\":\"CPU jitter on wall-clock; reporting median ± IQR and using Wilcoxon signed-rank rather than t-test.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"tracemalloc + psutil RSS are accepted memory proxies when torch.cuda.max_memory_allocated is unavailable.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"meta_gradient_ad is the primary apples-to-apples comparator for wall-clock and memory; implicit_meta_gradient_ad is a secondary branch because it changes the algorithm.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Numerical stability is operationalised as deviation from a fixed-step finite-difference reference hypergradient plus outer-loss drift across steps.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"TorchOpt v0.9.x (public at knowledge cutoff) is representative for API names.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Sandbox is not the execution env; a separate TorchOpt-provisioned env will run the experiment.</item>\\n\"}],\"uncertainties\":[{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":\"LOW: n=20 seed-power estimate is approximate; smaller-effect detection would need ~60 paired samples.</item>\\n\",\"$text\":\"LOW: No local TorchOpt source available — mechanism descriptions derive from public docs/paper, not from a read of /workspace.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"MEDIUM: Public TorchOpt has had minor API renames across versions; signatures above should be smoke-import-tested on the actual wheel before committing the experiment.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"MEDIUM: TorchOpt's transitive torch/functorch version pin is not verifiable here without network or a local wheel.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"MEDIUM: tracemalloc does not see PyTorch's CachingAllocator, so transient peak memory is under-estimated; RSS only gives process-wide envelope.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"HIGH: torch.func first-call tracing/JIT cost can dominate reported wall-clock if warmup is too short.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"HIGH: implicit_meta_gradient_ad and unrolled-autograd are not bit-equivalent at finite inner-step count; wall-clock/memory differences would conflate API with algorithmic change.</item>\\n\"}]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-11101ddf-30ed-4e6c-a88a-d6dc71f7c93c", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 12}
