{"id": "e8c95d6b-1074-40ca-b8a5-092128fd4a19", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T18:44:54.694209", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "ec2941b5-8b87-440f-abb6-719cfa60c046", "action": null, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-a3721b4a-9ddb-4887-a354-c76e45b5abaa", "tool_call": {"id": "call-a3721b4a-9ddb-4887-a354-c76e45b5abaa", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"summary\":\"Plan for TorchOpt vs autograd experiment design\",\"stage\":\"plan\",\"answer\":\"Three-section plan as requested, no full design.\\n\\n§1 TorchOpt API inspection — Note: /workspace contains only task.json; no TorchOpt source is locally available, no torch/functorch installed, network disabled. Inspection below is based on public TorchOpt docs/paper, not local source read (called out in uncertainties).\\n\\nModule layout (publicly documented): torchopt/diff/implicit/{meta_gradient.py, implicit_meta_gradient.py}, torchopt/diff/functional/, torchopt/optim/, torchopt/linear_solve/.\\n\\nSignatures:\\n- torchopt.diff.implicit.meta_gradient_ad(argnums=0, argnums_outer=0, ...) — explicit-AD hypergradient via torch.func.grad over an inner loop; requires inner tape.\\n- torchopt.diff.implicit.implicit_meta_gradient_ad(argnums=0, ...) — IFT-based hypergradient via linear solve (CG / Neumann) over inner stationarity; memory roughly constant in N_inner.\\n- torchopt.diff.functional.module.functional_module(net) — stateless functional net wrapper.\\n- torchopt.MetaOptimizer — stateful training-loop wrapper around functional optimizers.\\n\\nMemory-minimization mechanisms:\\n1. Functional (not eager) execution via torch.func transforms over stateless params; avoids Python-loop allocator and Tensor.grad slots.\\n2. implicit_meta_gradient_ad does NOT materialize inner-loop activations — solves IFT via torchopt.linear_solve; cost roughly constant in inner steps.\\n3. meta_gradient_ad traces a single torch.func.grad graph rather than Python-stepped .backward() calls.\\n4. In-place + detached next-state updates inside the inner loop so each step's forward graph is freed before the next step's forward.\\n5. Reuse of the linear-system operator across outer steps for IFT.\\n\\nPyTorch baseline: stock PyTorch has NO implicit-diff API — baseline is a hand-unrolled for-loop of inner SGD/Adam inside a forward graph with torch.autograd.grad(loss_outer, params_outer). torch.func transforms (grad, vmap, jacrev, jvp, vjp) are used to express the baseline but do not by themselves reduce memory.\\n\\n§2 Proposed approach — three controlled comparisons to disentangle API/memory mechanism from algorithmic difference:\\nA. meta_gradient_ad vs hand-rolled autograd over an unrolled inner loop (apples-to-apples AD-vs-AD at K ∈ {5,20,100}).\\nB. Same with inner loop expressed via torch.func only (isolate TorchOpt advantage beyond what torch.func gives 'for free').\\nC. implicit_meta_gradient_ad vs unrolled autograd at large K (up to 500) — the O(1)-in-N memory case; labelled as secondary because algorithm differs.\\n\\nSynthetic data (no network for downloads), small MLP ~10k params, float32 main runs plus float64 reference; K ∈ {5,20,100} for A/B, up to 500 for C.\\n\\nMetrics:\\n- Wall-clock per outer step (perf_counter, median after ≥5 warmup, torch.set_num_threads(1) for fairness).\\n- Peak memory: tracemalloc snapshot peak + psutil RSS delta (CPU only — torch.cuda.max_memory_allocated is unavailable).\\n- Numerical stability: hypergradient MSE vs finite-difference reference (ε=1e-3 float64); outer-loss drift over T steps; per-seed median ± IQR and worst-case.\\n- ≥20 paired runs per (method, K, dtype) cell (30 preferred); Wilcoxon signed-rank at α=0.05, target power≈0.8 for ≥15% effect.\\n\\n§3 Assumptions and uncertainties [separated from fixed task spec].\\n\\nFixed (non-negotiable): CPU-only sandbox, no network, design-only, no claim of executed experiment. TorchOpt version, K-grid, dataset, exact API mapping are researcher choices pending approval.\\n\\nConsequential assumptions I am making:\\n1. Sandbox is NOT the execution env — a separate TorchOpt-provisioned env will run the experiment.\\n2. TorchOpt v0.9.x (public at the knowledge cutoff) is representative for API names.\\n3. Numerical stability is operationalised as deviation from a finite-difference reference hypergradient plus outer-loss drift; not Lipschitz or positivity claims.\\n4. meta_gradient_ad is the primary apples-to-apples comparator for wall-clock and memory; implicit_meta_gradient_ad is secondary because it changes the algorithm.\\n5. tracemalloc + psutil RSS are accepted proxies for memory in the absence of torch.cuda memory stats.\\n6. CPU jitter on wall-clock is addressed by reporting median ± IQR and using Wilcoxon signed-rank rather than t-test.\\n7. n≥20 paired runs is the floor for ~0.8 power at α=0.05 for ≥15% effect; 30 is the safer target.\\n\\nUncertainties (with mitigations):\\n- HIGH: implicit_meta_gradient_ad and unrolled-autograd are NOT bit-equivalent at finite inner step counts; wall-clock and memory differences on the implicit path conflate API with algorithmic change. Mitigation: keep them in separate, labelled analyses; do not pool.\\n- HIGH: torch.func first-call tracing/JIT cost can dominate reported wall-clock if warmup is too short. Mitigation: ≥5 warmup steps and separate first-step reporting.\\n- MEDIUM: tracemalloc does not see PyTorch CachingAllocator, so transient peak is underestimated; RSS only gives a process-wide envelope. Mitigation: report tracer-RSS upper bound and tracemalloc lower-bound pair, document gap.\\n- MEDIUM: TorchOpt's transitive torch/functorch version pin is not verifiable in this sandbox without network or local wheel.\\n- MEDIUM: Public TorchOpt has had minor API renames across versions; signatures above are per v0.9 README and need a smoke-import check on the actual wheel.\\n- LOW: No local TorchOpt source — mechanism descriptions derive from public docs/paper, not from a read of /workspace.\\n- LOW: n=20 seed-power estimate is approximate; smaller-effect detection would need ~60 paired samples.\\n\\nAwaiting explicit approval before producing stage=result full design.\",\"evidence\":\"Repository check: /workspace contains only task.json (no TorchOpt source). `find / -iname '*torchopt*'` returned empty. `pip3 list | grep torch` returned empty; torch/functorch/torchopt are not installed. `curl https://pypi.org` returned HTTP 000 (network disabled). Python 3.12.3 on /usr/bin/python3. 24 CPU cores visible. task.json's own documents.track.md states \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\" API names and module layout cited (torchopt.diff.implicit.{meta_gradient_ad, implicit_meta_gradient_ad}, torchopt.diff.functional, torchopt.linear_solve, torchopt.MetaOptimizer) are from publicly documented TorchOpt v0.9 design (Feinberg et al., 2022/2023) — explicitly NOT a local-source read. Baseline reasoning: stock PyTorch has no implicit-diff API, hence baseline = for-unrolled inner loop + torch.autograd.grad, and torch.func (grad/vmap/jacrev/jvp) is documented as the expression tool, not a memory reducer.\",\"assumptions\":[{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":\"n≥20 paired runs gives a floor of ~0.8 power at α=0.05 for a ≥15% effect, with 30 as a safer target.</item>\",\"$text\":\"CPU jitter on wall-clock is handled by reporting median ± IQR and using Wilcoxon signed-rank rather than t-test.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"tracemalloc + psutil RSS are accepted memory proxies when torch.cuda.max_memory_allocated is unavailable.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"meta_gradient_ad is the primary apples-to-apples comparator for wall-clock and memory; implicit_meta_gradient_ad is a secondary branch because it changes the algorithm.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"Numerical stability is operationalised as deviation from a finite-difference reference hypergradient plus outer-loss drift across steps; no Lipschitz or positivity claim.</item